 "The compass is a device that relies on the magnetic properties of the Earth to indicate direction. The compass is composed of a metal needle made of a magnetic material such as iron or nickel. The needle is suspended within a magnetic field so that it is free to rotate. When the compass is placed near the Earth, the needle aligns itself with the Earth's magnetic field as it would in response to a magnetic force. The direction in which the needle aligns itself is the direction of the Earth's magnetic field at that location and is generally the same as the direction in which the compass indicates."
A museum note describing how a barometer works: "A barometer is an instrument used to measure the pressure of the air in a room. It is composed of a sealed glass tube filled with a clear liquid, typically mercury or water, and a floating chamber of air. As the air pressure changes, the level of the liquid in the tube changes. When the air pressure increases, the level of the liquid in the tube rises. Conversely,